WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.900
These two have a long, complicated history
and every time they meet it's always a coin

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.960
toss to see how they're going to react.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:07.960
Will they fight?

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.220
Will they work together?

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.920
Keep watching to learn more about the bizarre
relationship between Spider-Man and Venom.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.320
In 2007's Spider-Man 3, Peter Parker's bonding
with the Venom symbiote does more than change

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.869
the color of his costume and enhance his powers,
it makes him a jerk.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:27.550
The symbiote boosts destructive emotions in
Peter, like anger and jealousy.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.609
"When are you going to give a guy a break?"

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:34.390
"You want forgiveness?

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.390
Get religion."

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.149
Among other things, he hunts and attempts
to kill Sandman and brings Gwen Stacy to a

00:00:39.149 --> 00:00:42.600
nightclub in a manipulative plan to make Mary
Jane jealous.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.180
He even gets in a fight with the club's bouncers,
which ends with him accidentally hurting Mary

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.180
Jane.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:52.110
It's this last act that makes Parker realize
what's happening and finally take action to

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.860
get rid of the symbiote.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:57.079
In the comics, however, things worked out
a little differently.

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:01.149
While the symbiote made Spider-Man do things
against his will, it didn't do so by turning

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:03.649
him into an evil version of Peter Parker.

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:07.590
Rather than finding the costume on Earth like
he did in the film, Peter picked it up out

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.630
in space during the events of Secret Wars,
and shortly after he returned to New York

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.480
with his new suit, the symbiote apparently
decided that its host was a little too lazy.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:20.840
Most nights, the symbiote waited until Parker
was asleep, took over his body, and went out

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:25.420
to find more crime to fight, all the while
controlling its human host's unconscious body

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.900
like a puppet.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.720
One of the first clues Parker has that something
is wrong is when he constantly wakes up exhausted

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.000
even though, as far as he knows, he's been
asleep in bed for hours.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.920
While the symbiote doesn't turn Peter Parker
into a jerk in the comics, it's clear it impacts

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.160
his mental state in other, more subtle ways.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.560
How do we know?

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.399
Because it takes Peter, who's as much a scientist
as Bruce Banner and Tony Stark, a pretty long

00:01:49.399 --> 00:01:54.009
time to start examining a strange, unknown,
symbiotic life form that he picked up on an

00:01:54.009 --> 00:01:55.540
alien planet.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:59.399
Despite the fact that it produces its own
webbing and can shape-shift to look like anything

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.340
from a costume to regular human clothing,
he doesn't get around to figuring out what

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:07.159
it actually is until things start to go very,
very badly.

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:12.220
After picking up the alien costume in Secret
Wars #8, Peter returns to Earth in Amazing

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.150
Spider-Man #252.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:17.970
It's not until six months later that he finally
approaches the Marvel Universe's resident

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.870
super-scientific genius, the Fantastic Four's
Reed Richards, to help him figure out just

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.090
what's going on with his new costume.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:29.410
For months, Spider-Man simply accepts the
fact that he's wearing a freaky magical alien

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.420
suit he doesn't know anything about.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.770
Clearly, the costume's messing with his head.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:38.960
It's easy to argue that during Secret Wars,
where all of Marvel's most prominent heroes

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.730
and villains were brought to another planet
and forced to battle each other, Spidey was

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:46.920
desperate enough to just accept whatever he
could find to boost his chances of survival.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.060
Once he returns to Earth, though, maybe putting
that thing under a microscope should've been

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.960
his first priority.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.440
In 1985's Web of Spider-Man #1, the symbiote
betrays the emotional connection developing

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.530
between it and Peter Parker by revealing a
quality that you might not expect from a murderous

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.800
alien life form: compassion.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:10.840
While Peter is slipping on his classic red-and-blue
Spidey suit in his apartment, the symbiote

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.260
returns from being imprisoned in the Fantastic
Four's headquarters and attempts to bond with

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.640
him once again.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:20.810
Peter tries to return to the Baxter Building
to get help from the F.F., but the symbiote

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.630
does everything it can to keep him away from
the building.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:28.060
Eventually, Peter gets enough control to make
his way to a cathedral, taking advantage of

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.290
the symbiote's weakness to loud noises by
using the ringing of the church bells as a

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.290
weapon.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.630
In the process, Peter gets knocked out, and
while it initially slithers away to escape

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:41.910
the bells, the symbiote returns to save Peter
from the thunderous sound.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.860
The narration tells us that its bond with
Parker caused it to experience emotion for

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.370
the first time.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:51.960
Before disappearing, we see the alien reach
out, caressing Peter's cheek.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.520
In spite of the Venom symbiote bonding with
more than a dozen other characters since leaving

00:03:56.530 --> 00:04:01.720
Peter Parker, there are two behaviors that
show us how fixated it is on Spider-Man.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.570
First, there's its tendency to bond with people
in that are already in Peter's social circles.

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:10.680
Eddie Brock is the first and most obvious
example, since he was a rival photographer

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.860
at the Daily Bugle the first time he merged
with Venom.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.670
"Photography, it's not just about, no offense,
uh flagpoles, whatever, it's about lighting,

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:19.750
composition, drama."

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.590
Other hosts include Mac Gargan, better known
as the Spidey villain the Scorpion, but he's

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.090
not the only established bad guy to hook up
with the symbiote.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:32.840
In the pages of Superior Spider-Man, the comic
where Peter's identity was taken over by Doctor

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:37.810
Octopus, it temporarily bonded with Doc Ock's
brain in Spidey's body.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.380
After Brock, the man to have stayed with the
symbiote for the longest amount of time was

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:46.070
Peter Parker's former bully and best friend,
Flash Thompson, who used it to perform covert

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:49.520
missions for the government under the codename
Agent Venom.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.490
What's equally telling is how Venom manifests
visually.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:56.450
The symbiote has the ability to mimic any
set of clothing, but no matter who the host

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:00.790
is or where they fall on the moral spectrum,
Venom continues to base its appearance on

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:02.090
Spider-Man's.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.710
The eyes of its mask and the spider emblem
on its chest are clearly mirrors of Spidey's

00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:11.290
costume, as is the fact that it continues
to sling "webs" around, something other symbiotes

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:12.400
just don't do.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:17.221
Spider-Man and Venom don't exactly have the
best relationship, but occasionally, there's

00:05:17.221 --> 00:05:21.350
something bad enough to force them to work
together, and it usually comes from Venom

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:22.410
himself.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:26.610
More often than not, if Parker and Brock are
on the same team it's because one of Venom's

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:31.560
own spawn is up to no good, like the insane
and powerful villain Cletus Kasady, better

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:33.090
known as Carnage.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:41.960
"When I get outta here, and I will, there's
gonna be Carnage."

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:46.120
Venom and Spidey famously teamed up against
the villainous symbiote along with other Marvel

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:51.780
heroes in the 1993 event Maximum Carnage,
a hugely popular storyline that inspired the

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.760
classic 16-bit video game of the same name.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.230
There's a good reason why they had to team
up, too.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:02.300
Physically, Carnage is more powerful than
Spider-Man and Venom combined, and even more

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:03.300
durable.

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.810
Among other things, the villain has survived
being beheaded as well as being dragged into

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.000
space and ripped in half.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.140
Even worse, Kassady was already a convicted
serial killer before he got super-powers.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.810
When his cellmate, Eddie Brock, escaped from
prison, a scrap of the symbiote was left behind,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.090
and a terrifying new villain was born.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:25.460
In that respect, Carnage can be thought of
as Venom's "son," but the symbiotes clearly

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:29.330
don't have the same warm, fuzzy feelings the
rest of us have towards family.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.370
Still, the fact that Venom chose to stay on
Team Spidey when he had to choose between

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:38.280
siding with Peter Parker or teaming up with
his own spawn says a whole lot about his complicated

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.360
relationship with Spider-Man.

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.070
It's clear the Venom symbiote has complicated
feelings towards Peter Parker, but it's also

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:48.600
clear that those complex feelings are a two-way
street.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.050
Venom's place in the Marvel Universe is always
cycling between anti-hero and full-on villain.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:57.830
He can spend time as a less forgiving crimefighter,
as he did in a series of solo titles that

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:03.990
started in 1993 with Venom: Lethal Protector,
but cut to ten years later and 2003's Venom,

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.230
and he's right back to eating people for fun.

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:11.010
And yet, in spite of Venom's unpredictability
and his shifting loyalties, Spider-Man never

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:15.400
gets proactive about shutting him down, even
though he's the one with the great responsibility

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.050
of bringing the symbiote to Earth in the first
place.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:23.730
Instead, he's far more likely to form an uneasy
alliance with him, like he did to battle Carnage,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:27.030
and seems to hold out hope that the symbiote
could be redeemed.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:31.170
That might be a result of his bond with the
symbiote leaving him with an emotional connection,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:35.600
but there's plenty of evidence that Spidey
might be right after all.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.730
During its time as Agent Venom, the symbiote
spent a long time bonded to one of Parker's

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:45.230
closest pals, Flash Thompson, who, in addition
to being Peter Parker's best friend, was once

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.860
literally the president of the Spider-Man
fan club.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.350
"He's no Spider-Man."

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:50.580
"What is it with you and Spider-Man?"

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:51.580
"What?

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:54.250
He looks out for the neighborhood, has a dope
suit, and I really respect him!"

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:58.460
Thompson's Venom was a much more traditionally
heroic version of the character, and even

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.910
served alongside the Guardians of the Galaxy
and a covert squad of Avengers.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:07.760
While he was out in space, the symbiote revealed
a previously unknown tragic past, starting

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.090
out as a relatively peaceful member of an
alien species called the Klyntar before being

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:16.080
driven mad after mistakenly bonding with a
genocidal maniac.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.560
Thompson's innate sense of honor, however,
showed the symbiote a better way to live and

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.520
use its great power a little more responsibly.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.900
Whether that will stick remains to be seen,
but for now, Venom seems to be pretty firmly

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:30.210
on the side of the good guys.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:31.889
More or less.

00:08:31.889 --> 00:08:34.080
"You gonna eat anybody else?"

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:36.480
"Most likely!"

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:42.640
Miles Morales is probably best known these
days as the star of 2018's animated hit Spider-Man:

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:44.000
Into the Spider-Verse.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.610
In the comics, like in the film, Miles was
originally from an alternate universe to Peter

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:53.820
Parker, but was transported to the prime Marvel
Comics universe by a cosmic event that restructured

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:55.210
the multiverse.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:09:00.740
As a result, when Miles runs into Eddie Brock
in 2018's Venom #3, it's their first meeting,

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.340
and their introduction doesn't go well.

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:07.420
With his symbiote being controlled by the
dark god Knull, Venom comes close to killing

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:08.420
Miles.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:12.990
The younger Spider-Man manages to temporarily
incapacitate the symbiote, which gives Brock

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:14.670
takes over.

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:19.270
Eddie thanks Miles and tries to explain what
happened, but Miles doesn't give him the chance.

00:09:19.270 --> 00:09:24.370
Miles understandably presses the attack against
Eddie, and Venom is forced to fight back.

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:29.350
While Miles has never met Eddie Brock before
that issue, he isn't a stranger to symbiotes.

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:33.680
Back in his home universe, his life was thrown
into disarray by that world's version of Venom

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.210
in 2013, during a story appropriately titled
"Venom War."

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:42.630
Venom nearly killed Miles' father, and his
mother died as a result of the conflict.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:46.230
With that kind of history, it's easy to see
why Miles wouldn't be inclined to give Eddie

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:48.550
the benefit of the doubt once he was attacked.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:53.250
Eventually, in true Marvel Team-Up fashion,
the fight led to Miles and Eddie working together

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:54.880
for the next few issues.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.000
Time will tell what happens next time these
two fan-favorites cross paths.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:04.120
Marvel has been putting out issues of What
If? on and off since 1977, giving readers

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:08.120
the chance to experience what stories would
be like if they worked out differently.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:14.070
Usually, the answer is that things would be
pretty bad, but 1989's What If #4 takes things

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.570
to the next level.

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:19.990
The question this time around is "What if
the alien had possessed Spider-Man?" and things

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:24.920
wind up a whole lot worse for everyone, including
both Peter Parker and the symbiote.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.530
The Venom symbiote of What If?

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:31.540
#4 never develops the same emotional attachment
to Peter Parker as the main continuity's Venom

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:32.540
does.

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:36.550
Instead, it feeds on its host like a vampire,
draining Peter's youth and vitality in the

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:37.960
process.

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:42.320
Once Parker's life force is spent, the symbiote
starts looking for a bigger meal and chooses

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.480
the Hulk as his next host.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.300
It interrupts the massive battle between the
Hulk and the Avengers that readers had originally

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:53.210
seen in Incredible Hulk #300, and bonds with
the Green Goliath.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.500
As he does, Peter Parker's body is revealed
to have been prematurely aged, leaving him

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.310
as a withered husk.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:03.850
Peter dies not long after, and the Avengers
pursue the Venom-infected Hulk until the symbiote

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:06.830
trades up once again, leaving the Hulk's body
for Thor's.

00:11:06.830 --> 00:11:12.441
The heroes finally incapacitate Venom by getting
help from the Inhuman leader Black Bolt, whose

00:11:12.441 --> 00:11:17.070
deafening scream forces the symbiote from
Thor's body, and destroys Mount Rushmore in

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:18.070
the process.

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:21.960
Before the assembled heroes can banish the
symbiote to another dimension, the Black Cat

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:24.420
kills it in revenge for Parker's death.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:29.310
All things considered, it's a much less happy
ending than we got in the regular comics.

